data.content:
Your code **does not use `Thread.Abort`**, `Thread.Interrupt`, or catch `ThreadAbortException`. It only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — all of which work identically in .NET 8 with no migration needed.

**No changes are required** for the Thread.Abort migration concern.

That said, for modern .NET 8 best practices, you could modernize this to use `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.WhenAll` for cleaner parallelism:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
        {
            Thread.Sleep(100);
            item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
        });
    }
}
```

This gives you thread pool efficiency and built-in cancellation support (via `ParallelOptions.CancellationToken`) if you need it later.
